**Convention 1.2 (credited native interface).** Put

$$
C_0=\langle\alpha,\alpha\rangle,\qquad
S_1=\{\alpha,\beta,C_0\},\qquad
S_{d+1}=\{\alpha,\beta\}\sqcup\{\langle x,y\rangle:x,y\in S_d\}.
\tag{RCE.3}
$$

Use the credited temporal factors of [AAR, §§12–13]. Write
$s(a)=\#L(a)+2\#R(a)$ and $e(\alpha)=0,e(\beta)=1$. At an original
branch endpoint set $\Gamma_q(u)=(0,0)$. Otherwise let the first
original blocking leaf along $q$ have label $\ell$ at $a$, and put
$q=ab$. Then

$$
\Gamma_q(u)=
\begin{cases}
(2-e(\ell),1-e(\ell)),&b=\varepsilon,\\
(s(b)+2-e(\ell),\mathbf1_{b\text{ ends in }R}),&b\ne\varepsilon.
\end{cases}
\tag{RCE.4}
$$

For attained $(b,c)$, the supplied all-count decoder is

$$
\operatorname{Pred}((b,c),k)=
\begin{cases}
\mathsf{branch},&k\ge b,\\
\beta,&b\ge1,\ k=b-1,\\
\alpha,&c=1,\ k=b-2,\\
\mathsf{absent},&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\tag{RCE.5}
$$

This equals $r_{\rho^ku}(q)$ for every $k\ge0$. Its trajectory
recovers $b$ as the first branch count and $c$ by the occurrence of an
alpha stage. Initially absent targets are included.

At depth $d$, write $g_q=\Gamma_q$ and $r_a(x)=r_x(a)$ for
$a,q\in W_d$, and include a constant experiment. FMR canonicalization
$\pi_d$ preserves these replies and factors and fixes $S_d$.
Every finite word on $S_d$ is a function of one boundary reply in the
same block, or a constant: a length-$d$ reply is alpha exactly when
its length-$(d-1)$ parent is branch, and longer words are absent.
This is the every-word simulation of [FMR, Lemma 2.2].

